.auction__bar
  flex 0 0 auto
  background $backgroundColor

.auction__bar-body
  flex 0 0 auto
  height 70px
  display flex
  max-width fit-content

// `min-width 0` because a flex item defaults to `min-width auto`, which refuses
// to shrink below its content and so converts any shortfall into overflow of
// the BAR rather than compression of this panel. With the election control in
// the strip the bar body ran 1445px inside a 1000px cap, putting the last
// button past the right edge of the viewport.
// The settlement status is NOT here. It names every team still to answer, so
// its length grows with the league and has no natural upper bound -- inside a
// 70px bar that made it either the thing setting the bar's width or, once
// bounded and clamped, a box with its own sentence cut off. It lives in
// `.auction__side` alone, where it is a paragraph that may take the height it
// needs. Nothing about it belongs in the bar's stylesheet.
.auction__bid-info
  flex 1
  min-width 0
  display flex

.auction__bid-actions
  flex 0 0 auto
  display flex
  align-items center

.auction__main-input
  flex 0 0 auto
  position relative
  height 100%
  input
    border none
    width 60px
    height 100%
    text-align center
    font-size 20px
    padding-top 12px
    background #fafafa
    font-family 'IBM Plex Mono', monospace
    &::-webkit-inner-spin-button
    &::-webkit-outer-spin-button
      -webkit-appearance none
      margin 0
  label
    absolute top 4px right
    text-align center
    width 60px
    font-size 9px
    font-weight 300
    text-transform uppercase

// NO `min-width` ON THE BUTTONS, and the rule that used to be here had never
// applied. It read `.button { min-width 150px }`, which is 0,2,0; button.styl
// declares `min-width 30px` on
// `.button:not(.MuiButton-root):not(.MuiIconButton-root):not(.MuiFab-root):not(.MuiButtonBase-root)`,
// which is 0,5,0 and wins outright. Every button on this row computes 30px and
// sits at its content width -- measured at 320: the stepper's icons at 40 each,
// `Bid $1` at 56.3, `Decline` at 65, `Change maximum` at 125.5. It was removed
// rather than made to work: a 150px floor on five buttons is 750px on a row that
// cannot afford 535.
.auction__main-action
  flex 0 0 auto
  padding 0 16px
  display flex
  align-items center
  gap 8px

.auction__main-timer
  position relative
  flex 0 0 60px
  height 100%
  display flex
  justify-content center
  align-items center
  user-select none

.auction__text
  font-weight 600
  font-size 1.5em
  display flex
  align-items center
  .team__name
    line-height 1.5em

// The stepper goes green while you hold the winning bid. `winning` is pushed
// onto the group's className in auction-main-bid.js; it reached the buttons
// through MUI's own group classes until the group became @components/button-group.
.button-group.winning > .button
  +button_override()
    background rgb(46, 125, 50)
    color rgba(255,255,255,0.8)

@media (min-width 1160px)
  .auction__bid-actions
    height 100%
    background white

// 1120px rather than 1000px, and the breakpoint moves with it so the cap is
// never wider than the viewport that opts into it.
//
// The cap is what decides whether the nominated player fits. `.auction__bid-actions`
// is fixed-content (timer, stepper, election control, bid input) and took 593px
// measured, so the bar's cap minus that is all `.auction__bid-info` ever gets.
// At 1000px that was 407px for 468px of content -- a 264px name beside a 204px
// details group -- and since both are flex items that had run out of room to
// shrink, the surplus became overlap: "MARKET $0" printed on top of "PICKETT".
// Giving the bar the extra 120px is the one fix here that costs no content;
// every alternative was dropping a detail column or clipping the player's name
// on the surface whose subject IS that player.
@media (min-width 1160px)
  .auction__bar-body
    max-width 1120px
    margin 0 auto

// EVERYTHING BELOW THE CAP IS THE STACKED LAYOUT, and 1160 is one boundary for
// both facts because they are the same fact. The cap is what decides whether the
// single row fits: `.auction__bid-actions` is fixed content and measures 551px,
// so below the cap -- where the bar gets the raw viewport -- the player half is
// whatever is left, which measured 233px at 1024, 109px at 900 and 29px at 820
// against the 502px it needs. The row cannot be honest there, so it stacks.
//
// This used to switch at 800, which is why 800-1160 was a documented broken
// zone rather than a layout. Below the cap the bar also runs edge to edge, so
// the text needs its own gutter in the same breath.
@media (max-width 1159px)
  .auction__text
    padding 0 16px

  // THE OTHER BRANCH OF THE SAME SLOT, and it never got the gutter. `main` in
  // auction-main-bid.js is either `.auction__text` above -- eight message states
  // -- or `.auction__nominated-player`, and below the cap the bar runs edge to
  // edge so both need one. Only the first had it, so `Live value` sat flush
  // against the right edge of the screen at every stacked width.
  //
  // ONLY THE LEFT HALF LIVES HERE. The right gutter is unconditional and is
  // declared on the base rule in auction-nominated-player.styl, because the
  // desktop row needs it too -- there it separates this group from
  // `.auction__bid-actions` rather than from the screen.
  //
  // 8px rather than 16, because `.player__name-headshot` already carries
  // `margin-left 8px` in this zone and the two add up. The pair is 16 and 16
  // either side, matching `.auction__text` above it.
  .auction__nominated-player
    padding-left 8px

  .auction__bar
    display flex
    align-items center
    justify-content center

  // `min-height`, not `height`. Stacked, this box owes its two halves the sum
  // of what they need, and a FIXED height silently resolves the shortfall by
  // shrinking whichever half will give -- it does not report it. At 768 the
  // halves asked for 105 + 46 = 151px of a 120px box and the player half was
  // crushed from 105 to 74; at 600 the controls wrap to two lines and grow to
  // 64px, the player half has nothing left to give, and `.auction__bid-actions`
  // spilled 13.4px out the BOTTOM of the bar onto the team chips below. 120px
  // is still the right resting height and every width but 600 sits at it; the
  // floor just lets the one width that needs more take it instead of painting
  // outside itself.
  // `height auto` IS LOAD-BEARING and is easy to leave out. The base rule at
  // the top of this file sets `height 70px`, which the old `height 120px` here
  // overrode outright. Swapping that for `min-height` alone leaves the 70px
  // standing: the floor clamps the used value up to 120, so the box measures
  // 120 and looks correct, but it is still a DEFINITE height and definite
  // heights do not grow for their content. Measured in that state at 600px --
  // rect 120, scrollHeight 133, the spill entirely intact behind a number that
  // read as fixed. `auto` is what makes the min-height a floor rather than the
  // whole answer.
  // `align-items stretch`, NOT `flex-start`, AND THAT ONE WORD IS THE WHOLE OF
  // the player half being narrower than the bar it sits in. Stacked, this box is
  // a COLUMN, so `align-items` governs the children's WIDTH -- and `flex-start`
  // sizes each row to its own content instead of to the column. The bar's width
  // is set by the wider of the two rows, the actions, so the player half was
  // left at its content width with the difference as dead space on its right:
  // measured at 768, a 583px bar with a 402px player half, 181px short; at 600,
  // 489 against 398. `flex 1 0 auto` on `.auction__bid-info` cannot fix it,
  // because grow distributes free space along the MAIN axis and this is the
  // cross one.
  //
  // The 181px is also what the name and the details were fighting over. They are
  // both flex items inside that half, so a half narrower than it needs to be is
  // where "the details group cuts into the player" comes from -- and a box
  // overlap check reads clean through it, because the boxes tile while the ink
  // does not.
  // `max-width 100%` REPLACES THE BASE RULE'S `fit-content`, and this is the
  // whole of the bar running past both edges of a phone. `fit-content` resolves
  // to the box's MIN-CONTENT when that exceeds the space available, and the
  // actions row's min-content was 535.1px flat -- so at 420, 372, 360, 320 and
  // 300 the bar body measured 535.1 at every one of them, centred by the
  // `justify-content center` above and therefore hanging off BOTH sides: 82px
  // each at 372, 118px at 300.
  //
  // `doc_overflow_x` READ 0 AT ALL OF THOSE WIDTHS. `body` is `overflow hidden`
  // on this page, so the overflow is clipped rather than scrolled and the
  // document reports nothing. The read that sees it is the element's rect
  // against the viewport -- `-rect.left` and `rect.right - vw`. A document-level
  // overflow check is not evidence of anything here.
  //
  // The cap alone would only move the failure inside the box; the wrapping below
  // is what lets the content answer it.
  .auction__bar-body
    flex-direction column
    height auto
    min-height 120px
    align-items stretch
    max-width 100%

  // The 12px of top padding is the room the bid input's label paints into --
  // see `.auction__main-input` below, whose label sits above its box and out of
  // flow. Without it the label would print over the bottom of the player
  // details, which end where this row begins.
  .auction__bid-actions
    padding 12px 16px 8px
    justify-content center
    width 100%
    // THE ROW WRAPS RATHER THAN SWITCHING LAYOUT AT A BREAKPOINT, and that is
    // deliberate: it is self-regulating, so there is no width to pick and no
    // second layout to keep in step. It needs 535.1px on one line -- 32px of
    // padding, 443.1px of stepper and election control, and the 60px bid input
    // -- and below 800px the bar gets the raw viewport, so the line stops
    // fitting below 536px and only there.
    //
    // No amount of slimming reaches 300px on one line. The row's content is
    // 503.1px against a 268px budget there, and the buttons are already at their
    // content width (see .auction__main-action above); the timer is not even
    // rendered in election mode. Wrapping is the only thing that spans the gap.
    flex-wrap wrap
    row-gap 10px

  // No `height 105px`, and no `position relative` with it. The height was a
  // fiction -- this box's contents measure 69px, so the extra 36px existed only
  // to be shrunk away, and the shrinking is what left no room for the headshot
  // and forced it out of the bar. Sized to its contents the two halves come to
  // 69 + 46 = 115px, which fits the 120px above with room to spare. The
  // `position relative` was an anchor for the floated headshot and nothing else
  // in here is absolutely positioned, so it went with it.
  //
  // `flex 1 0 auto` RATHER THAN INHERITING THE BASE `flex 1`, and deleting the
  // fixed height is not enough without it. `flex 1` is `flex 1 1 0%`: a basis of
  // ZERO, which means this box reports no content to the column's height
  // calculation and is then handed whatever is left over. That reproduces the
  // exact defect the fixed height had -- at 600px the bar would size itself to
  // 120px knowing only about the 64px controls and hand this box the remaining
  // 56px for 69px of content. `auto` makes the basis the content, so the bar's
  // height accounts for both halves; `0` shrink stops the leftover being taken
  // back out; `1` grow lets it absorb the 5px of slack at 768 rather than
  // leaving a dead strip under the controls.
  .auction__bid-info
    flex 1 0 auto

  // `flex 0 1 auto` AND `min-width 0`, replacing the base rule's `flex 0 0
  // auto`. Shrink, but never grow: growing would stretch this group across the
  // row and push the bid input to the far edge at every stacked width, changing
  // a layout that is correct from 536 up. `min-width 0` removes the `auto`
  // minimum that otherwise refuses to shrink below content and converts the
  // shortfall into overflow rather than into a wrap.
  //
  // It wraps internally as well as being wrappable, so the stepper takes one
  // line and the election control the next once they stop sharing one.
  .auction__main-action
    flex 0 1 auto
    min-width 0
    flex-wrap wrap
    justify-content center
    row-gap 10px

  .auction__main-timer
    border-radius 8px
    overflow hidden

  // THE ONLY CONTROL ON THIS ROW WITH A VISIBLE BOX, so it is the only one
  // whose top edge the eye can line up -- and it was the one that did not line
  // up. `height 100%` stretched it to the whole row while its neighbours are
  // 26px buttons, giving a 38px box whose top sat 5.8px above theirs at 768 and
  // further at 600. Their CENTRES agreed the whole time, which is why a centre
  // check reads clean and the defect is still plainly there in a screenshot.
  //
  // The extra 12px was never the number, it was the label: `.auction__main-input
  // label` is absolutely positioned over the input's own `padding-top 12px`, so
  // the box had to be tall enough to hold a caption drawn on top of it. Put the
  // label in normal flow ABOVE the box and the box only has to hold the number,
  // which is exactly the shape `Your election` / `$5` already uses two controls
  // to the left. The label lands at that label's height and the box lands at
  // the buttons' height, so the row reads as one line instead of four controls
  // and a tall panel.
  //
  // Above 800px none of this applies: the bar is a single 70px row, the input
  // fills its full height by design, and the label has the room to sit inside.
  // THE LABEL MUST NOT COST HEIGHT, which is the whole difficulty. Putting it
  // in normal flow above the box makes a 37px stack, and a 37px stack centred
  // on a row of 26px buttons puts the box 5.7px BELOW them -- the same defect
  // mirrored, which is what the first attempt at this shipped into the browser.
  // The box can only land on the buttons' band if it is the only thing the
  // layout sizes, so the label goes back out of flow and paints ABOVE the box
  // rather than on top of it, and the row is given the padding to receive it.
  .auction__main-input
    height 26px
    align-self center

    label
      // Directly above the box, not inside it. `bottom 100%` is relative to
      // this same box, so the two move together if the height ever changes.
      top auto
      bottom 100%
      right 0
      padding-bottom 2px

    input
      height 26px
      padding-top 0

// THE PHONE LAYOUT, and it replaces wrapping with a single scrolling line.
//
// 535 is measured, not chosen: `.auction__bid-actions` needs 535.1px on one line
// and below 800px the bar gets the raw viewport, so this is exactly the zone
// where the row cannot be honest. Above it nothing here applies and the row is
// the one line it has always been.
//
// THREE MOVES, and they only make sense together:
//   1. the bid input goes, which is 60px and the only box on the row nobody can
//      reach with a thumb without a keyboard covering half the screen;
//   2. `Your election` takes its own line, laid out along it rather than
//      stacked, which costs one 15px line instead of a 27px block beside the
//      buttons;
//   3. what is left -- the stepper, `Decline` and `Change maximum` -- stays on
//      ONE line and scrolls sideways when it does not fit, rather than wrapping
//      to a third row.
//
// Together they take the chrome at 320 from 312px to a little over half the
// screen's worth less. Wrapping was correct as a first answer and is the wrong
// one here: a phone bid bar that grows a row every time a label does is a bar
// that eats the board it exists to sit under.
@media (max-width 535px)
  // GONE, AND THIS IS THE ONE ITEM HERE THAT COSTS SOMETHING. Without it a
  // manager on a phone reaches a bid amount with the stepper's plus and minus
  // rather than by typing it, which is more taps for a large jump. The trade is
  // deliberate: it is 60px of a 268px budget, it is the only control on the row
  // that opens a keyboard over the auction it is bidding in, and every amount is
  // still reachable.
  .auction__main-input
    display none

  // THE 12px OF TOP PADDING WENT WITH THE BID INPUT, because it was never this
  // row's padding -- it was the room `.auction__main-input label` painted into,
  // sitting above its box and out of flow. The input is gone here, so the label
  // is gone, so the reservation for it is 12px of a phone's chrome held for
  // nothing.
  //
  // 8px each side rather than 16. The row scrolls, so its horizontal edge is a
  // scroll boundary rather than a line the eye aligns anything to, and 16 was
  // costing 32px of the widest thing on the screen. The gutter lives HERE and
  // not on the scrolling box below on purpose: padding-right on a scroll
  // container is not honoured at the end of the scroll range in Chrome, so a
  // gutter written there disappears exactly when it is doing something.
  .auction__bid-actions
    padding 0 8px 8px

  // THE SCROLLING ROW. `flex 1 1 100%` so it takes the bar's width to scroll
  // INSIDE, and `min-width 0` so it is allowed to be narrower than its content
  // -- without it the box grows to fit and there is nothing left to scroll.
  .auction__main-action
    flex 1 1 100%
    min-width 0

  // GRID, NOT FLEX, and the reason is that flex cannot do this. The two things
  // that have to happen -- one item on its own line, the rest on a single line
  // that scrolls -- are `flex-wrap wrap` and `flex-wrap nowrap` on the same box,
  // and a wrapped line cannot scroll because it wraps instead. Grid places the
  // state on its own row by name and leaves the second row a single line.
  //
  // `max-content` columns are what make the second row OVERFLOW rather than
  // compress, which is what there is to scroll. `auto` or `1fr` would shrink the
  // buttons to fit and there would be no overflow and no scrollbar -- the
  // content would simply be squeezed, which is the defect, not the fix.
  //
  // `justify-content start`, NOT `center`. A centred track whose content
  // overflows puts the overflow on BOTH sides, and the left half is then
  // unreachable -- a scroll container cannot scroll to a negative offset. The
  // row reads left-aligned under a left-aligned player name above it.
  .auction__bid-actions .auction__main-action
    display grid
    grid-template-columns max-content max-content
    // Two rows only, and the election control's note deliberately gets no area
    // of its own here even though it becomes an item of THIS grid at this width
    // (its body is `display contents` below 535px). A declared third row is a
    // declared third `row-gap` -- 6px added to a bar with none to give, in every
    // state, to reserve space for a line that appears in one. The note spans the
    // columns from its own stylesheet instead, which creates the row implicitly
    // and only when the note exists.
    grid-template-areas 'state state' 'stepper form'
    justify-content start
    align-items center
    row-gap 6px
    column-gap 8px
    // No horizontal padding: the gutter is on `.auction__bid-actions` above,
    // outside the scroll. See the note there.
    padding 0
    overflow-x auto
    // The row scrolls; the bar does not. Without this the vertical axis becomes
    // `auto` the moment the horizontal one stops being `visible`, and the bar
    // grows a second scrollbar it has no height to show.
    overflow-y hidden

  .auction__bid-actions .button-group
    grid-area stepper

  .auction__main-timer
    flex 0 0 30px

// Lives in `.auction__bid-info` beside the settlement status, not inside the
// 60px timer box it used to share a row with. Given the same shape as the
// status it alternates with, so the slot does not jump between live and
// election mode.
.auction__block-label
  flex 0 0 auto
  align-self center
  margin-right 10px
  padding 4px 8px
  font-size 10px
  line-height 1.3
  text-transform uppercase
  letter-spacing 0.5px
  opacity 0.7

// The nominate action and its OPTIONAL ceiling, side by side. The field is
// election-mode only: inside a live block the engine already proxies every
// standing maximum and the bid clock closes the player, so a ceiling attached
// to a nomination there would duplicate the election control.
.auction__nominate
  display flex
  align-items center
  gap 6px

.auction__nominate-maximum-bid
  width 96px
  padding 4px 6px
  font-size 12px
